<!--Post: 3618--><!--Time: 1105065394-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3618"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Curious2<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Curious2<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 6:36 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->1.  No random swabbing since mid-December. <BR>&#40;Remember they were swabbing police, media, and current arrestees. It was a major swabathon!&#41; <BR>   <BR>2.  No press releases since mid-December until a &#34;mini year-end briefing&#34; by Police Chief. There did not seem to be a sense of urgency in asking the public for more tips.  He almost said that as an after-thought, kind of like, &#34;Oh yeah, by the way, we&#39;re still seeking tips from the public.&#34;  <BR> <BR>3. No info released on Murdock package that was sent to FBI. <BR> <BR>4.  Re: Nancy Fox&#39;s Pearl Necklace Press Release.  Profilers state that a life-altering event occured earlier this year in order for BTK to emerge again.  Are police hopeful it could have been a divorce after 25 years?  IOW, if they show the pearl necklace, would the now &#34;ex-wife&#34; be more apt to turn him in, if she had received it as a gift when they were merely dating? <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3618-->
<!--Post: 3625--><!--Time: 1105070806-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3625"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iceteajan<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iceteajan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 8:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Curious2 <BR> <BR>Weren&#39;t there a few who refused to give specimens? <BR> <BR>I would definitely zero in on those guys. <BR> <BR>Can&#39;t they get specimens OTHER ways &#40;a cigarette thrown away, a paper cup used and tossed, etc.&#41;?? <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3625-->
<!--Post: 3648--><!--Time: 1105081805-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3648"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dpinva<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dpinva<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 11:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Something significant changed in the way WPD is behaving, at least in public, since the Murdock Park package was discovered. I can&#39;t help thinking that there was something significant in, or about, that package that has enabled them to, at the very least, focus on a particularly fruitful line of investigation. Perhaps BTK finally screwed up and left some kind of forensic evidence &#40;a print, fiber, who knows&#41; or was seen, or something. This entire episode searching for Miss Fox&#39;s necklace indicates to me that there was some kind of valuable information in the Murdock Park package. <BR>I might be way off, but for them to bring up the necklace NOW indicates that they just learned about it NOW, rather than 26 years ago. <BR>Perhaps BTK himself said something in the letter he left or left something unique in the package that has given Lt. Landwehr and his boys a solid lead.  <BR>Bottom line in my opinion is that something seems to have been learned, and the police are not exactly sure where this knowledge will lead yet. Hence thier silence about the package and in general. It smells like they have a particular point of focus on a particular suspect, or if not a single person at least a unique charateritic that weeds the list out considerably.  <BR>There might be a lot going on behind the scenes that we do not yet know about. Lets hope that it leads to the truth and the justice for the victims and thier families, and to a fub filled vacation resort courtesy of the Kansas Department of Corrections for BTK.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3648-->
<!--Post: 3652--><!--Time: 1105084553-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3652"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Skov<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Skov<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 06, 2005 - 11:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hi Dpiva <BR> <BR>Maybe Nancy Fox was wearing the necklace on the picture on the driver licence? And untill police saw that picture they didn´t know about the necklace. Maybe BTK gave som kind of puzzle/clue in that package that somthing missing in the package is located around somone elses neck? <BR> <BR>skov<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3652-->
<!--Post: 3720--><!--Time: 1105159972-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3720"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 8:52 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I do not think the police have anybody they consider to be a strong suspect if they did they would have acted by now. I think you would have seen something akin to the taking in and questioning of the gentleman months ago. He wasn&#39;t a strong suspect but they had no problem bringing him in and taking dna samples for comparison to btks. I believe the necklace being asked for now is not because of something in the package left in the park. I think just the opposite. The police believe BTK is &#34;walking us through each of them&#34;&#40;his murders&#41;with these new communications. I believe he is doing just that with each new package he returns the items from each scene. The police have the belief this necklace was taken. If it was not returned but her license and other things were returned in this last package, why not the necklace? The answer because he doesnt have it. Did he lose it? Not likly he didnt lose anything else? He gave it to somebody? This is much more probable becaue serial killers are known to do this. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3720-->
<!--Post: 3732--><!--Time: 1105162789-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3732"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 9:39 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Zeroed in on a suspect? Probably not. Might they have one or two suspects who seem promising but there isn&#39;t enough information yet? Yes. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3732-->
<!--Post: 3734--><!--Time: 1105163233-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3734"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Curious2<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Curious2<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 9:47 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, George.  How long does it take to gather <BR>all the pertinent information on a suspect?  I&#39;m <BR>sure it is a lengthly process - especially in <BR>order to avoid a debacle like in early December! <BR>After many hours of research of a potential <BR>suspect, how do you think WPD would try to arrest <BR>him this time?  Surely not like the early December arrest at his home, do you think? Rather, do you think they would have him under <BR>surveillance and wait until he makes the Otero <BR>anniversary drop? <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3734-->
<!--Post: 3738--><!--Time: 1105163774-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3738"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 9:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Curious2 you make a good point about not wanting a repeat of the past arrest and being more careful the next time. However, I don&#39;t see the change in their behavior some see. With this last press release, it seems they are still looking for that one person to come forward they haven&#39;t heard from yet that has that little bit of missing info that connects BTK to his crimes. I just don&#39;t think, they think ,they have any solid <BR>suspects.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3738-->
<!--Post: 3747--><!--Time: 1105172044-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3747"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jujuree<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jujuree<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 12:14 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think the answer is in between what George and Scbenson said. <BR> <BR>I do think they have one or two &#40;or more&#41; suspects in mind.  They may seem very solid, in a circumstantial sort of way AND they&#39;re waiting for a good tip to, hopefully, point them towards one of those suspects. <BR> <BR>Seeing how many &#34;suspects&#34; onliners have come up with based on circumstantial evidence alone, I wonder if the WPD doesn&#39;t have a long list of potential BTK&#39;s.  They need a good tip to single one of them out or else they&#39;re looking for a needle in a haystack. <BR> <BR> <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3747-->
<!--Post: 3751--><!--Time: 1105176097-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3751"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 1:21 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If they had any solid suspects they would collect dna samples and we would have an arrest. No new swabbing has been done recently. Any swabbing already done they have the results already. They will not make an arrest until that DNA is matched. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3751-->
<!--Post: 3753--><!--Time: 1105179269-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3753"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 2:14 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think they&#39;ve got a handful of guys - I&#39;m not going to guess at a number - who all look very good, who fit the parameters they are looking for. Guarantee you that at least one of them has exercised his right to not be swabbed.  <BR> <BR>I base this on an interesting thing I noticed. If you take what was known before with what the WPD revealed about BTK on Nov.30, and go on a thorough and imaginative google hunt, honestly, you can come up with at least 2 to 4 men in the space of a night whose backgrounds in ham radio, railroading, and appearances - if you are lucky enough to find a photo - raise the hairs on the back of your neck.  <BR> <BR>The name Larry Anderson, for instance. Someone was freaking at the C&amp;J board about a poster on ks.com who calls himself Larry, even though the dude has never posted anything that makes him sound suspicious, other than his screen name being chosen, according to him, because of that &#34;puzzle&#34;. <BR> <BR>But I found a real Larry who, at first, looked way too good - ham radio, railfan, age right, military record - decorated - only problem was, as far as I could determine, this guy had lived in a state hundreds of miles from Kansas all his life, and from what I saw, didn&#39;t have a mobile job, either <i>&#40;trucker, salesguy&#41;</i>.  <BR> <BR>My point is; if you or I can come up with people in a google search that bother us with their similarities to what we &#34;know&#34; about BTK, just think of what it&#39;s probably like for WPD detectives, who have <b>everything</b> available at their disposal? Can you imagine how frustrating that is? <BR> <BR>Now that I&#39;ve written all that I see Jujuree has said the same thing with far fewer words. C&#39;est la vie. Eh. I&#39;ll leave it up anyway.  <BR> <BR>If they do have a short list, the strongest prospects I&#39;m certain are being surveilled in some fashion. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m curious, if they do indeed have good suspects, about how closely they are able to follow, say, computer transactions. Computer surveillance gets more scarily sophisticated everyday, and if they know he&#39;s &#34;monitoring&#34;, I wonder if they&#39;ve planted trojans or subpoenaed his ISP to eyeball his tracks through servers. <BR> <BR>Good, chunky food for thought, all of it.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3753-->
<!--Post: 3763--><!--Time: 1105198406-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3763"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Curious2<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Curious2<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 7:33 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks, Steve.  But what does &#34;planted <BR>trojans&#34; mean?  Of course, I understand <BR>from the Greek epic, the Odyssey, &#34;beware of Greeks bearing gifts&#34;, but how does that <BR>apply here?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3763-->
<!--Post: 3781--><!--Time: 1105228897-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3781"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jujuree<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jujuree<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 4:01 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Scbenson, that&#39;s what I&#39;m saying.  The WPD may have many suspects who &#42;seem&#42; solid, but only in a circumstantial way.  That probably isn&#39;t enough to force anyone to be swabbed for DNA.  A judge might not issue a warrant based on such general things like age or where they lived or if they like trains.  Those criteria would match too many people, I would think.  The WPD would probably have to come up with something more that specifically singles out the suspect they want to DNA test, like someone calling in a tip about that person in regards to the necklace.   <BR> <BR>I keep hearing that there have been several men who have refused to volunteer for a DNA swab.  I don&#39;t know if that&#39;s true, but if it is, a tip called in about one of them could legitimize a warrant.  I hope that&#39;s what&#39;s going on and this necklace stuff isn&#39;t just a totally blind attempt at information, with no suspects in mind.  I hope the WPD are further along than that.  &#40;Not holding my breath.&#41;  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3781-->
<!--Post: 3806--><!--Time: 1105253044-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3806"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 08, 2005 - 10:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Curious2, specific information about interview techniques from Quantico, combined with WPD&#39;s experience would, in my opinion, guarantee an excellent interview as to any suspect brought in. The hard part is finding the suspect. I wouldn&#39;t be shocked if there is no Otero anniversary drop-off as BTK is feeling the heat. Either way, whether a drop-off occurs or not, it&#39;s more behavioral information to work with. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3806-->
<!--Post: 3944--><!--Time: 1105442154-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3944"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nugzter<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nugzter<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 11, 2005 - 3:15 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Come on guys. It is actually very simple. I bet a millon bucks they know who he is, have talked to him and relatives. Maybe left their card for him to call. He wont give his DNA using the excuse that it would compromise the rights of his familys privacy. &#40;dna from father is passed to kids&#41; They ae putting the pressure on this one person and have pretty much made up their minds. Of coure WPD cant act until they have something in the hand to link him to the murders. So they turn up the heat till he quits. <BR>Just like the Green River Killer. They had GRK pegged for years, just no hard evidence. They turned up the heat and he cracked. Full confession, plus some unsolved murders. Thats the tactic, makem sweat so much they slip up or crack up. Alot of people will be eating their words when it coms to WPD. Could be they were on this guy for years and now after the resurfacing, they know for sure. Just need the evidence. Could be that the Park packag was his last bit of evidence he needed to get rid of. Now its gone. Only the necklace remains, and he don&#39;t have that. Matter of a few months, or never. Maybe a death bed confession. Doubt it. He wants to win. He has lost the monster, why be punished for something he himself did not do. At least in his mind.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3944-->
<!--Post: 3947--><!--Time: 1105444991-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3947"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 11, 2005 - 4:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I wish I had a million, I&#39;d see your bet. In Kansas, can one legally avoid submitting DNA because it compromises the right&#39;s of one&#39;s family? Your opinions seem sound but if WPD had a prime suspect, I really think they could -- and would -- find a way to get DNA. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3947-->
<!--Post: 3951--><!--Time: 1105447501-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3951"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nugzter<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nugzter<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 11, 2005 - 4:45 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Not true. Probable cause is hard to pove in a murder case. They just cant come to you with a warrent and demand your DNA. If it came out that the probable cause was not justified the DNA evidence would be thrown out of court and the convection is lost. By the ay thee ae 5 WPD former officers who also refuse to submit DNA samples cause of the family reason.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3951-->
<!--Post: 3952--><!--Time: 1105448332-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3952"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 11, 2005 - 4:58 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>By the ay thee ae 5 WPD former officers who also refuse to submit DNA samples cause of the family reason.</i> <BR>Where can I find this information?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3952-->
